{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Fast ADD/address ALU bypass\n===========================\n\nWhat changed\n------------\n- Added an EX-local `fast_add_result` in `rtl/ex_stage.sv`, using the same\n  forwarded operands already selected for the ALU.\n- `reg_q.alu_result` now comes from a priority mux:\n  1. JAL/JALR return address (`pc + 4`)\n  2. iterative DIV/REM result\n  3. fast ADD/address result\n  4. generic ALU result\n- Left `rtl/alu.sv` functionally intact, including `ALU_ADD`, so standalone\n  ALU tests and any non-EX reuse still behave the same. EX no longer depends\n  on the generic ALU operation mux for ADD, ADDI, AUIPC, or load/store\n  effective addresses.\n- Added focused ADD-style vectors and extra MUL edge vectors in\n  `test/test_alu.py`.\n- Added a pipeline test covering AUIPC plus signed load/store address adds in\n  `test/test_pipeline.py`.\n\nDeviations\n----------\n- I did not remove `ALU_ADD` from `alu.sv`; keeping it preserves the existing\n  unit-level ALU contract while the timing-oriented bypass happens in EX.\n\nConcerns\n--------\n- Branch instructions carry the decoder default `ALU_ADD`, so they also select\n  the fast add value for the otherwise-unused EX/MEM `alu_result`. This is not\n  architecturally visible because branches do not write a register or access\n  memory, and branch compare/target logic is unchanged.\n- No vendor timing run was performed.\n\nLocal verification\n------------------\n- Verilator lint: pass\n  `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`\n- Formal: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed\n  `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`\n- Changed cocotb tests: pass, 2 passed\n  `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_alu.py cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`\n- Cosim: pass, 2 ELFs tested\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`\n\nNote: plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` built the harness with its default\n`NRET=2` and failed before simulation because this core declares `nret: 1` and\ndoes not expose channel-1 RVFI ports. Rerunning with `NRET=1` matches\n`cores/bench/core.yaml` and passed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
