{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Ahead-registered direct predictor\n\nWhat changed:\n- Added `ahead_direct_predictor.sv`, a four-entry exact-PC direct-control predictor with registered lookup output, aligned targets, and 2-bit saturating confidence.\n- Added a one-bit `predicted_taken` marker to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\n- Updated IF to select between `pc+4` and the predictor's registered target while preserving EX redirect priority, and to prelookup the PC that will be fetched next cycle.\n- Added one top-level outstanding predicted-target register. New predictions are blocked until the marked instruction resolves in EX.\n- Updated EX to suppress redirects only when a predicted control instruction resolves to the outstanding target; stale predictions, loop exits, misaligned targets, and target mismatches redirect through the existing flush path.\n- Trains only accepted, resolved taken backward conditional branches and direct JALs. JALR is intentionally excluded.\n- Added a focused cocotb unit test for registered predictor lookup timing and exact-PC behavior. Existing core-level cocotb source lists were updated to include the new predictor RTL.\n\nDeviations:\n- Used four entries rather than two. The lookup result is registered before IF consumes it, so the extra entries should mainly cost small table state rather than frontend mux depth.\n- The single outstanding target is a top-level register rather than a field carried in the pipeline structs, preserving the requested one-bit instruction metadata.\n\nConcerns:\n- Back-to-back predicted control transfers are intentionally throttled: while one prediction is unresolved, later predictor hits are ignored. This keeps verification state small but leaves some performance on the table for dense jump chains.\n- Confidence is only strengthened on taken training events; loop exits recover by redirect but do not decay the entry.\n\nLocal checks:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_ahead_direct_predictor.py`: pass.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: pass, 2 ELFs tested.\n- Note: bare `make cosim TARGET=bench` fails in this checkout because the cosim build script defaults to `NRET=2`, while `cores/bench/core.yaml` declares `nret: 1`. The orchestrator normally supplies the matching NRET.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
