{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'rg -n \"direct|predict|branch\" cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/CORE_PHILOSOPHY.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative divider off ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline timing report shows the worst path entering the EX forwarding\\nmuxes and then the monolithic ALU. The current ALU keeps RV32M DIV/REM in\\nthe same combinational result mux as ADD, branch compare support, and memory\\naddress generation, so every common integer operation pays timing for a rare\\nslow operation. CoreMark's hot path is dominated by simple ALU, load/store,\\nand branch work, making the divider a high-value target for a focused\\ntiming micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline timing report shows the worst path entering the EX forwarding\\nmuxes and then the monolithic ALU. The current ALU keeps RV32M DIV/REM in\\nthe same combinational result mux as ADD, branch compare support, and memory\\naddress generation, so every common integer operation pays timing for a rare\\nslow operation. CoreMark's hot path is dominated by simple ALU, load/store,\\nand branch work, making the divider a high-value target for a focused\\ntiming micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of the combinational ALU and into a small\\niterative divide/remainder unit controlled by EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\\ncompare, multiply, load/store address, and jump return generation on the\\nexisting single-cycle path, but assert an EX-local stall only while a divide\\nor remainder instruction is in flight. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, preserve\\nthe existing combinational substitute formulas so the fast formal checks keep\\ntheir current behavior. Removing the divider cone from the normal ALU mux\\nshould substantially improve Fmax while adding cycles only for rare RV32M\\ndivision instructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 25, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the normal combinational ALU path while retaining RV32I operations, MUL vari\n…[91585 of 94657 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1227ab25ee83ac79]…\nremove.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:12:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r4s2 (regression, -34.78%): Moving predictor lookup off the IF PC-update mux is not enough if every instruction still carries a 32-bit predicted_next_pc into EX for resolved_next_pc comparison and recovery, because the verification metadata can cost more Fmax than CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:15:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, -12.99%): A one-outstanding loop predictor still regresses after removing per-instruction predicted_next_pc payloads because the IF full-PC tag/target mux and EX outstanding-record compare/redirect gating remain on CoreMark's hot control path.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:18:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r6s2 (regression, -0.12%): Even with one-bit `predicted_taken` metadata and registered training, an exact direct-branch/JAL predictor was only break-even because IF full-PC tag/target selection plus EX redirect gating still cost about as much as CoreMark's saved taken-control bubbles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
