{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n    if not line.strip(): continue\n    o=json.loads(line)\n    if o.get('category')=='predictor' or 'predict' in o.get('title','').lower() or 'BTB' in json.dumps(o):\n        print('ID', o['id'])\n        print('title', o['title'])\n        print('outcome', o.get('outcome'), 'fitness', o.get('fitness'), 'delta', o.get('delta_pct'), 'fmax', o.get('fmax_mhz'), 'cycles', o.get('cycles'))\n        hyp=o.get('hypothesis')\n        if isinstance(hyp, dict):\n            print('hypothesis:', hyp.get('hypothesis','').replace('\\\\n',' ')[:1000])\n        else:\n            print('hypothesis:', str(hyp)[:1000])\n        print('lesson', o.get('lesson',''))\n        print()\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "ID hyp-20261003-003-r1s2\ntitle Static direct-control-flow predictor\noutcome regression fitness 12.63 delta 4.21 fmax 5.378 cycles 4259699\nhypothesis: Add a decode-time static predictor in fetch for direct control flow: predict JAL as taken to its immediate target and predict conditional branches with a backward offset as taken while leaving forward branches and all JALR/returns predicted not-taken. Carry predicted_taken and predicted_target with the instruction into EX, where the already-existing branch/jump resolution compares actual next PC against the predicted next PC and redirects only on mismatch. Correctly predicted backward loop branches and direct JALs should retire with no redirect bubbles; only loop exits, forward taken branches, JALR, and mispredictions pay the current recovery cost. \nlesson - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, +4.21%): A fetch-stage static direct-control predictor can lose more to the combinational imem_data-to-PC path through immediate decode/target add/mux than it saves in redirect bubbles, so keep predictor target selection off the PC-update critical path unless taken-branch density justifies it.\n\nID hyp-20261003-003-r2s2\ntitle Tiny PC-indexed BTB predictor\noutcome regression fitness 88.53 delta -15.37 fmax 37.483 cycles 4234021\nhypothesis: Add a very small direct-mapped BTB/BHT updated in EX and consulted in IF by PC bits only. Each entry stores a valid bit, a short tag, a predicted target, and a 2-bit taken counter or unconditional bit. Fetch chooses either pc+4 or the stored target on a tag hit without looking at imem_data, so the failed imem_data-to-immediate-to-PC combinational path from the prior predictor is avoided. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX; EX computes the architectural next PC for branches and jumps, updates the predictor, and only redirects/flushes when predicted_next_pc differs from the resolved next PC. This should improve iterations per cycle on hot CoreMark loops with a modest predictor table cost and much lower Fmax risk than d\n…[3130 of 6202 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:776940b2fe8c9101]…\n that next instruction is fetched, the PC register advances through a short mux fed by the already-registered predicted next PC rather than by a live tag/table/ counter/target path. Use two very small direct-mapped entries trained by EX on taken backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets, and carry only predicted_next_pc plus a valid/taken bit with the instruction. EX already computes the resolved next PC, so it can redirect only when the latched prediction differs and can update the entries on real control-flow outcomes. This targets the cycle reduction seen in the BTB and loop-predictor trials while keeping the IF PC path close to the current pc+4-or-redirect mux. \nlesson - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r4s2 (regression, -34.78%): Moving predictor lookup off the IF PC-update mux is not enough if every instruction still carries a 32-bit predicted_next_pc into EX for resolved_next_pc comparison and recovery, because the verification metadata can cost more Fmax than CoreMark's saved redirect bubbles.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
