{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | \\\"### \\\\(.id) \\\\(.title) [\\\\(.outcome)]\\\\nHYP: \\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nLESSON: \\\\(.lesson // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "### baseline-bench-6f5defa Baseline retest for cores/bench [improvement]\nHYP: null\nLESSON: \n\n### hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 Iterative divider off ALU critical path [improvement]\nHYP: Move DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of the combinational ALU and into a small\niterative divide/remainder unit controlled by EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\ncompare, multiply, load/store address, and jump return generation on the\nexisting single-cycle path, but assert an EX-local stall only while a divide\nor remainder instruction is in flight. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, preserve\nthe existing combinational substitute formulas so the fast formal checks keep\ntheir current behavior. Removing the divider cone from the normal ALU mux\nshould substantially improve Fmax while adding cycles only for rare RV32M\ndivision instructions.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 (improvement, +763.12%): Pulling rare DIV/REM out of the ALU result mux removed divider timing from CoreMark's simple ALU/load-store/branch hot path, so an EX-local iterative stall can buy large Fmax gains when division is cold.\n\n### hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 Valid-predicated load-use interlock [improvement]\nHYP: Replace redirect/imem-stall NOP injection with valid-bit bubble handling in\nID, and make the load-use interlock source-aware. Fetch should pass the raw\nimem word plus a valid bit; ID should zero the ID/EX bundle whenever it\ncaptures an invalid fetch. The decoder should provide compact uses_rs1 and\nuses_rs2 predicates, and hazard_unit should compare a load destination only\nagainst architecturally live source operands while also ignoring invalid\nIF/ID work. This removes the EX redirect -> IF instruction mux -> decode /\nhazard dependency from the ID/EX control path and avoids false stalls on\nimmediate instructions whose imm[4:0] happens to match a preceding load rd.\nThe existing one-cycle true load-use interlock, dmem hold behavior, DIV/REM\nbusy stall, and nret=1 RVFI contract remain unchanged.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +10.53%): Replacing redirect\n…[10840 of 13912 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b373053ebbc4d548]…\nath slimming [regression]\nHYP: Make redirect flushing single-owner: keep the existing hazard-unit redirect\ninput as the mechanism that bubbles ID/EX, but remove redirect from the\nif_stage output-valid calculation and from the redundant next_pc mux. Fetch\nshould still give redirect priority for the PC register, while IF valid only\nreflects instruction-memory availability. This should cut an EX branch/jump\ncompare-to-IF-valid-to-ID-control combinational path without adding branch\npenalty, prediction state, bypass sources, or new stalls. Because it is a\npure control-path simplification, CoreMark IPC should remain unchanged while\nFmax has a chance to move past the current placement-sensitive plateau.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r13s0 (regression, -4.82%): Removing redirect from IF `out.valid`/`flush_if` and the redundant `next_pc` mux did not help because redirect still drives the timing-critical PC update and ID/EX `flush_id` bubble, so this core's redirect pressure is not in the IF-valid kill path.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
