{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\(.outcome) fitness=\\\\(.fitness) delta=\\\\(.delta_pct) fmax=\\\\(.fmax_mhz) cycles=\\\\(.cycles)\\\\nHYP: \\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTE: \\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "ID: baseline-bench-6f5defa\nTITLE: Baseline retest for cores/bench\nOUTCOME: improvement fitness=12.12 delta=0.0 fmax=5.442 cycles=4491485\nHYP: null\nNOTE: \n\nID: hyp-20261003-001-r1s0\nTITLE: Iterative divider off ALU critical path\nOUTCOME: improvement fitness=104.61 delta=763.12 fmax=46.99 cycles=4491817\nHYP: Move DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of the combinational ALU and into a small\niterative divide/remainder unit controlled by EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\ncompare, multiply, load/store address, and jump return generation on the\nexisting single-cycle path, but assert an EX-local stall only while a divide\nor remainder instruction is in flight. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, preserve\nthe existing combinational substitute formulas so the fast formal checks keep\ntheir current behavior. Removing the divider cone from the normal ALU mux\nshould substantially improve Fmax while adding cycles only for rare RV32M\ndivision instructions.\n\nNOTE: \n\nID: hyp-20261003-001-r2s0\nTITLE: Valid-predicated load-use interlock\nOUTCOME: improvement fitness=115.63 delta=10.53 fmax=51.933 cycles=4491402\nHYP: Replace redirect/imem-stall NOP injection with valid-bit bubble handling in\nID, and make the load-use interlock source-aware. Fetch should pass the raw\nimem word plus a valid bit; ID should zero the ID/EX bundle whenever it\ncaptures an invalid fetch. The decoder should provide compact uses_rs1 and\nuses_rs2 predicates, and hazard_unit should compare a load destination only\nagainst architecturally live source operands while also ignoring invalid\nIF/ID work. This removes the EX redirect -> IF instruction mux -> decode /\nhazard dependency from the ID/EX control path and avoids false stalls on\nimmediate instructions whose imm[4:0] happens to match a preceding load rd.\nThe existing one-cycle true load-use interlock, dmem hold behavior, DIV/REM\nbusy stall, and nret=1 RVFI contract remain unchanged.\n\nNOTE: \n\nID: hyp-20261003-001-r3s0\nTITLE: Fast ADD/address ALU bypass\nOUTCOME: regression fitness=110.16 delta=-4.73 fmax=49.477 cycles=4491402\nHYP: Add \n…[3139 of 6211 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5a9f43f34a9d7455]…\nn earlier attempts.\n\nNOTE: \n\nID: hyp-20261004-001-r7s0\nTITLE: ID-normalized ALU operands\nOUTCOME: regression fitness=121.39 delta=-2.05 fmax=54.519 cycles=4491320\nHYP: Normalize the execution operands for LUI, AUIPC, and other source-less\ninstructions in ID, while preserving raw instruction source fields for RVFI.\nCarry separate compact execution source addresses/values in ID/EX: force\nsource-less operands to x0, preload AUIPC's execution rs1 value with pc, and\npreload LUI's execution rs1 value with zero. With those operands already\ncanonicalized, EX can use the forwarded rs1 value directly instead of a\n32-bit is_auipc ? pc : rs1 mux, and ALU_LUI can be removed or folded into\nADD with rs1=0 and b=imm. Because the forward unit still compares ordinary\nfive-bit execution source addresses rather than extra uses_rs gates, this\nkeeps the hot forwarding control as shallow as the current champion while\ntrimming one ALU input mux and one ALU result-select arm from the common\nCoreMark address/add/branch path.\n\nNOTE: \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
