{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r10s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r10s1\ntitle: \"Zero-wait load-use bypass\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion has already peeled DIV/REM, high multiply, low MUL, and\n  load/store address generation away from the ordinary EX result path, leaving\n  smaller timing-only opportunities. CoreMark still pays an unconditional\n  one-cycle bubble for every true load-use pair even though the benchmark memory\n  model often returns the load word in the very next MEM cycle. Linked-list and\n  state-machine code tends to consume freshly loaded values quickly, so removing\n  that structural bubble can improve iterations/cycle without adding predictor\n  machinery to the fragile IF/redirect path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a MEM-to-EX load-data forwarding lane and change the load-use interlock so\n  a dependent instruction may enter EX directly behind a load. When the load is\n  in MEM and io_dmemReady is high, MEM computes the sign/zero-extended load data\n  combinationally and forwards it to the EX operand muxes with priority over the\n  older MEM/WB path. If the data bus stalls, the existing dmem stall already\n  freezes EX/MEM and ID/EX until the load completes, so correctness is preserved\n  without the unconditional front-end bubble. This targets a direct CoreMark IPC\n  loss that prior MEM-stage early writeback did not address, while avoiding new\n  PC prediction, store buffering, or full execute-stage retiming.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire a MEM-stage load-forward valid/rd/data channel into EX and the forwarding unit without changing the top-level IO or nret=1 RVFI contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose the current load's sign/zero-extended data and destination as a combinational forward source when the EX/MEM instruction is a valid aligned load.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a MEM-load operand-forward input and extend the rs1/rs2 muxes so a dependent instruction can consume load data while the producer is in MEM.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Prioritize EX/MEM load-data forwarding for matching source registers, while keeping ordinary EX/MEM ALU and MEM/WB forwarding behavior unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Remove the unconditional load-use bubble and rely on the existing dmem stall to hold the dependent instruction only when the load data is not ready.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Update focused pipeline coverage so zero-wait load-use pairs retire without the old mandatory bubble and stalled loads still hold correctly.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
