nal substitute formulas so the fast formal checks keep\\ntheir current behavior. Removing the divider cone from the normal ALU mux\\nshould substantially improve Fmax while adding cycles only for rare RV32M\\ndivision instructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 25, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the normal combinational ALU path while retaining RV32I operations, MUL vari\n…[167097 of 170169 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:46e7a0332b8ddc7f]…\nthe filtered CoreMark taken-control hits to repay.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:28:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r10s0 (regression, -5.84%): Splitting rare JAL/M-sidecar writeback values into `side_result` can regress when the saved EX/MEM `alu_result` input mux reappears as a `side_result_valid` select on the hot EX/MEM forwarding and WB paths.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:29:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r10s1 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait load-use bypass cannot just force `load_use_bubble` off; random iStall+dStall exposes consumer cycles without a valid MEM load result, so any MEM-to-EX load lane needs a ready-qualified interlock across combined bus stalls.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:30:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r10s2 (regression, -31.76%): Registering a direct predictor's lookup ahead of fetch still leaves `pred_outstanding_valid_q`/`predicted_taken` and the EX `predicted_target == actual_redirect_target` check on the redirect/PC-select path, so CoreMark's saved taken-branch bubbles cannot repay the added control depth.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
