{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r14s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r14s1\ntitle: \"Decode-stage MEM bypass capture\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is timing-limited by the RF/IDEX source path through the\n  EX operand muxes, compact ALU, and EX/MEM alu_result register. Prior attempts\n  show that predictors, wider ALU recoding, and serializing visible RV32I work\n  usually cost more than they save, while the successful changes have removed\n  specific hot-path mux inputs without changing CoreMark-visible control flow.\n  The remaining structural target is the MEM/WB forwarding leg that every EX\n  operand still carries even though most distance-two dependencies can be\n  resolved one cycle earlier during decode.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a decode-stage bypass-capture path from the instruction currently in MEM\n  to the source values being latched into ID/EX. MEM should expose a\n  post-trap, post-load-extension writeback candidate for its in-flight\n  instruction; ID uses that candidate when IF/ID rs1 or rs2 matches, otherwise\n  it keeps the existing write-first regfile read. With distance-two ALU, load,\n  jump, and completed M results already captured in ID/EX, the EX-stage\n  forwarding network can drop the MEM/WB data leg and keep only the immediate\n  EX/MEM bypass needed for producer-consumer pairs.\n\n  This should shorten the common EX operand path from a three-way\n  ID/EX-versus-EX/MEM-versus-MEM/WB mux to a two-way mux, and remove the\n  MEM/WB compare/control fanout from the timing-critical ALU setup cone. It\n  preserves the same pipeline latency, load-use bubble policy, dmem stall\n  behavior, RVFI source values, and nret=1 top-level contract, so CoreMark\n  cycles should remain essentially unchanged while Fmax has a plausible path\n  above the 4.6% replacement threshold.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose a combinational MEM-stage writeback candidate after load extraction and misalignment/trap gating for decode-stage source capture.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"When latching ID/EX source operands, prefer the MEM-stage writeback candidate on rs1/rs2 matches before falling back to the existing write-first regfile data.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Simplify EX forwarding to the immediate EX/MEM producer only, since distance-two results are captured in ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Replace the current three-way operand forwarding muxes with two-way ID/EX-or-EX/MEM selects on the hot execute lanes.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the MEM-stage bypass candidate into ID and remove MEM/WB from the EX forwarding unit without changing top-level IO.\"\n  - file: test/test_decode_mem_bypass.py\n    description: \"Add dependency tests for ALU/load/JAL/M results consumed at distance two, dmem stall replay, store-data capture, and immediate predecessor EX/MEM forwarding.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
