{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n \\\"category: structural|\\\\\\\"category\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"structural\\\\\\\"|structural|split|stage|pipeline|retire|bypass|forward|regfile|IF/ID|ID/EX|EX/MEM|MEM/WB\\\" cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative divider off ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline timing report shows the worst path entering the EX forwarding\\nmuxes and then the monolithic ALU. The current ALU keeps RV32M DIV/REM in\\nthe same combinational result mux as ADD, branch compare support, and memory\\naddress generation, so every common integer operation pays timing for a rare\\nslow operation. CoreMark's hot path is dominated by simple ALU, load/store,\\nand branch work, making the divider a high-value target for a focused\\ntiming micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline timing report shows the worst path entering the EX forwarding\\nmuxes and then the monolithic ALU. The current ALU keeps RV32M DIV/REM in\\nthe same combinational result mux as ADD, branch compare support, and memory\\naddress generation, so every common integer operation pays timing for a rare\\nslow operation. CoreMark's hot path is dominated by simple ALU, load/store,\\nand branch work, making the divider a high-value target for a focused\\ntiming micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of the combinational ALU and into a small\\niterative divide/remainder unit controlled by EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\\ncompare, multiply, load/store address, and jump return generation on the\\nexisting single-cycle path, but assert an EX-local stall only while a divide\\nor remainder instruction is in flight. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, preserve\\nthe existing combinational substitute formulas so the fast formal checks keep\\ntheir current behavior. Removing the divider cone from the normal ALU mux\\nshould substantially improve Fmax while adding cycles only for rare RV32M\\ndivision instructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 25, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the normal combinational ALU path while retaining RV32I operations, MUL vari\n…[79996 of 83068 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:21f62f4447a8e369]…\nresult-priority selection on the EX/MEM input path, implying the generic ALU ADD mux was not the binding CoreMark timing cost.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:8:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, +0.59%): A fall-through IF prefetch FIFO can fail to repay its FIFO/dequeue/bypass/redirect-control cost on CoreMark when random imem bubbles and backend stalls are not long or independent enough to amortize added fetch-side control.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:10:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 (regression, +6.28%): Splitting single-cycle MUL into a parallel mul_unit can regress when the added EX/MEM result-priority mux and mul_op decode outweigh removing multiplier arms from the simple ALU case tree.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:14:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r5s1 (regression, -9.03%): A split EX1/EX2 stage does not buy timing if CoreMark's 1/cycle ALU chains are preserved with an EX2-result-to-EX1 bypass, because the producer ALU -> forwarding mux -> EX1 register cone remains hot and load-use interlocks get longer.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
