{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r6s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Nonblocking RAM store buffer\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current in-order pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a memory\n  operation in MEM sees io_dmemReady low. Loads must block because they produce\n  rd data, but ordinary RAM stores have no register result and CoreMark runs\n  with about 22 percent random data-bus backpressure. A tiny store buffer is a\n  structural way to recover cycles from store-side dmem bubbles without adding\n  predictor metadata or another ALU bypass path to the known timing-critical\n  execute lane.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one- or two-entry nonblocking store buffer behind MEM for normal RAM\n  stores only. When a store reaches MEM and the data bus is not ready, capture\n  its address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI fields, let the store\n  retire, and keep driving the buffered request until io_dmemReady accepts it.\n  Loads, subsequent stores when the buffer is full, and all MMIO/marker stores\n  must wait until older buffered stores drain so memory ordering, CoreMark\n  timing markers, and UART behavior remain exact. This should improve\n  iterations per cycle by turning many store-only dmem stalls into background\n  work while keeping the common ALU, branch, load-use, and low-MUL paths close\n  to the current champion.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a compact store-buffer entry type or metadata fields for buffered RAM store address, data, mask, and ordering state.\"\n  - file: rtl/store_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Add a small FIFO/skid buffer that drives pending RAM stores to dmem until io_dmemReady accepts them and reports full/empty status.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Enqueue eligible RAM stores on dmem stalls, keep MMIO and marker stores blocking, and force loads to wait behind older buffered stores.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Differentiate blocking memory stalls from store-buffer enqueue cases so IF/ID/EX freeze only when a load, MMIO store, or full buffer requires it.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire store-buffer status and dmem ownership between MEM and the global stall logic without changing the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_store_buffer.py\n    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for buffered RAM stores across dmem stalls, load-after-store ordering, full-buffer stalls, and unbuffered MMIO marker stores.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
