{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,260p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 (improvement, +763.12%): Pulling rare DIV/REM out of the ALU result mux removed divider timing from CoreMark's simple ALU/load-store/branch hot path, so an EX-local iterative stall can buy large Fmax gains when division is cold.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +699.67%): Moving all RV32M ops behind a pipeline-stalling multicycle unit can erase timing wins because CoreMark uses MUL in hot loops, so isolate cold DIV/REM before making multiplier latency visible.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, +4.21%): A fetch-stage static direct-control predictor can lose more to the combinational imem_data-to-PC path through immediate decode/target add/mux than it saves in redirect bubbles, so keep predictor target selection off the PC-update critical path unless taken-branch density justifies it.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +10.53%): Replacing redirect/imem-stall NOP injection with IF/ID valid bubbles and gating load-use checks by decoder uses_rs1/uses_rs2 trims the ID/EX control path while avoiding CoreMark false stalls from immediate bitfields that alias a prior load rd.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, +4.39%): A real IF/ID register adds a taken-redirect recovery cycle even when the PC updates immediately, so CoreMark's branch density can outweigh the timing relief from breaking the redirect/imem/hazard/ID/EX control cone.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, -15.37%): Even PC-only BTB/BHT prediction can regress when the IF PC-update path grows a combinational table read, tag check, counter test, and 32-bit target mux that CoreMark's avoided redirect bubbles do not repay.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (regression, -4.73%): An EX-local ADD/address bypass can regress when it duplicates the