{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r3s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r7s1\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nHYP:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"ID: hyp-20261003-001-r3s0\nTITLE: Fast ADD/address ALU bypass\nHYP:\nAdd a dedicated fast result path in EX for ALU_ADD-class operations and\nmemory address generation. Compute the 32-bit add result beside the existing\nALU and let EX select it directly for ADD, ADDI, AUIPC, loads, and stores,\nwhile the generic ALU continues to handle logic, shifts, set-less-than, LUI,\nand one-cycle MUL variants. Keep DIV/REM on the existing iterative path and\npreserve all RVFI fields. This should shorten the common EX/MEM input path by\navoiding the full ALU operation mux on the most frequent CoreMark operations,\nimproving Fmax without adding cycles or changing branch recovery behavior.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\nTITLE: Split execute into operand and ALU stages\nHYP:\nSplit the execute stage into EX1 and EX2. EX1 performs operand selection,\nforwarding, branch comparison, jump target calculation, and early redirect;\nEX2 receives registered operands/control and performs the simple ALU,\nlow-half MUL, load/store address generation, and the existing cold sidecar\nresult acceptance before handing off to MEM. Add EX2-to-EX1 and MEM/WB-to-EX1\nbypassing so ordinary ALU dependency chains still issue at one instruction\nper cycle, while load-use and long sidecar hazards continue to interlock.\nBecause branch redirect remains in EX1 rather than moving later, the common\ntaken-loop penalty should stay close to the champion, but the critical path\nis divided into a forwarding/compare half and an ALU/result half. The expected\ngain is a higher Fmax with limited CoreMark cycle impact.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20261004-002-r7s1\nTITLE: MEM-stage early writeback\nHYP:\nMove the architectural register-file write port from the WB stage back to\nthe MEM stage, while keeping MEM/WB as the RVFI retirement and rd_wdata\nbookkeeping boundary. ALU, jump-link, and completed load results should be\nwritten when the instruction successfully leaves MEM; dmem stalls hold the\nwrite off, and decode/branch/memory traps still suppress it. The existing\nwrite-first regfile bypass then lets an instruction in ID see a producer that\nis completing MEM in the same cycle, including the normal one-bubble\nload-use case. With that early writeback in place, remove the MEM/WB operand\nforwarding leg and leave only EX/MEM forwarding for immediately preceding\nALU producers. This shrinks each EX source mux from three choices to two and\ndeletes the MEM/WB compare/select logic from the critical execute path\nwithout adding CoreMark cycles.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
