{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r9s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r9s1\ntitle: \"Split memory address execute lane\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst timed cone still runs from the execute operand\n  path through the general ALU toward memory-observed state. Previous structural\n  wins came from moving expensive arithmetic out of the ordinary EX lane, while\n  frontend predictors, extra full execute staging, store buffering, and regfile\n  replication all added too much control or placement cost. Loads and stores are\n  frequent in CoreMark and their effective address is the one ALU result that is\n  directly observed by the FPGA wrapper's dmem path, so shortening that lane is\n  a focused structural target.\nhypothesis: |\n  Partition EX/MEM into a dedicated effective-address lane plus the existing\n  architectural writeback-result lane. Compute load/store addresses with a\n  narrow rs1-plus-immediate adder and carry that value in a new EX/MEM address\n  field consumed by MEM for dmem_addr, masks, misalignment checks, and RVFI\n  memory address reporting. Keep ordinary ADD/logic/compare/shift/writeback\n  results on the existing ALU lane, and keep MUL/DIV/MULH sidecars unchanged.\n  Unlike the earlier fast ADD bypass attempt, do not add a priority mux in front\n  of the single alu_result field for every operation; instead remove memory\n  addressing from the full ALU/result-selection mux altogether. If the current\n  timed path is dominated by address-producing loads/stores, this should raise\n  Fmax without changing branch policy, memory handshakes, or CoreMark IPC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a dedicated effective-address field to ex_mem_t so memory operations no longer consume the general alu_result payload.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute and register a load/store effective address beside the normal ALU result, while leaving sidecar control and RVFI source capture unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the new effective-address field for dmem_addr, byte-lane masks, misalignment checks, and RVFI aligned memory address reporting.\"\n  - file: test/test_memory_address_lane.py\n    description: \"Add focused load/store tests covering byte, halfword, word, and misaligned accesses through the split address lane.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
