{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_22", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'rg -n \"tagless|no tag|untagged|tag-free|full PC tag|full-PC tag|direct-mapped\" cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny PC-indexed BTB predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempt showed that decoding imem_data in fetch and\\nadding a branch immediate target directly into the PC update path can destroy\\nFmax. However, CoreMark still has enough loop branches and direct control\\ntransfers that avoiding EX redirect bubbles is attractive if the prediction\\npath is kept short. A predictor indexed only by the current PC should preserve\\nmost of the champion's timing while recovering cycles on repeated taken\\nbranches and jumps.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempt showed that decoding imem_data in fetch and\\nadding a branch immediate target directly into the PC update path can destroy\\nFmax. However, CoreMark still has enough loop branches and direct control\\ntransfers that avoiding EX redirect bubbles is attractive if the prediction\\npath is kept short. A predictor indexed only by the current PC should preserve\\nmost of the champion's timing while recovering cycles on repeated taken\\nbranches and jumps.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped BTB/BHT updated in EX and consulted in IF by\\nPC bits only. Each entry stores a valid bit, a short tag, a predicted target,\\nand a 2-bit taken counter or unconditional bit. Fetch chooses either pc+4 or\\nthe stored target on a tag hit without looking at imem_data, so the failed\\nimem_data-to-immediate-to-PC combinational path from the prior predictor is\\navoided. Carry the predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX; EX computes the\\narchitectural next PC for branches and jumps, updates the predictor, and only\\nredirects/flushes when predicted_next_pc differs from the resolved next PC.\\nThis should improve iterations per cycle on hot CoreMark loops with a modest\\npredictor table cost and much lower Fmax risk than decode-time prediction.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\n…[41594 of 44666 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1b6bcbfff05a2b7d]…\nores/bench/LESSONS.md:15:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, -12.99%): A one-outstanding loop predictor still regresses after removing per-instruction predicted_next_pc payloads because the IF full-PC tag/target mux and EX outstanding-record compare/redirect gating remain on CoreMark's hot control path.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:18:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r6s2 (regression, -0.12%): Even with one-bit `predicted_taken` metadata and registered training, an exact direct-branch/JAL predictor was only break-even because IF full-PC tag/target selection plus EX redirect gating still cost about as much as CoreMark's saved taken-control bubbles.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:21:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r7s2 (regression, -25.18%): Extending the exact direct-control predictor to all taken branch directions can turn CoreMark's forward conditionals into costly wrong-path fetches, so IF full-PC tag/target muxing plus EX `predicted_taken` recovery needs stronger bias filtering than a tiny direct table provides.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
