\tOutstanding loop target predictor\tregression\t-12.99%\t46.497\t4312007\t47\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, -12.99%): A one-outstanding loop predictor still regresses after removing per-instruction predicted_next_pc payloads because the IF full-PC tag/target mux and EX outstanding-record compare/redirect gating remain on CoreMark's hot control path.\nhyp-20261004-003-r6s2\tExact direct-branch taken predictor\tregression\t-0.12%\t53.069\t4287237\t12\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r6s2 (regression, -0.12%): Even with one-bit `predicted_taken` metadata and registered training, an exact direct-branch/JAL predictor was only break-even because IF full-PC tag/target selection plus EX redirect gating still cost about as much as CoreMark's saved taken-control bubbles.\nhyp-20261004-003-r7s2\tAll-direction direct branch predictor\tregression\t-25.18%\t39.784\t4290383\t66\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r7s2 (regression, -25.18%): Extending the exact direct-control predictor to all taken branch directions can turn CoreMark's forward conditionals into costly wrong-path fetches, so IF full-PC tag/target muxing plus EX `predicted_taken` recovery needs stronger bias filtering than a tiny direct table provides.\nhyp-20261004-003-r8s2\tTiny return-address predictor\tregression\t-30.15%\t38.761\t4478020\t75\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r8s2 (regression, -30.15%): Even a return-only RAS predictor can lose badly when the IF return-PC filter/RAS-target mux and EX outstanding-return compare/squash logic lengthen the hot redirect path more than CoreMark's JALR return bubbles repay.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
