{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-003-r6s2\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r3s2\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r5s2\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-003-r6s2\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r3s2\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r5s2\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "ID: hyp-20261003-003-r3s2\nTITLE: Single-entry loop-back predictor\nMOTIVATION:\nThe previous predictor attempts proved there are enough taken control-flow\nbubbles in CoreMark to save roughly five percent of cycles, but they also\nshowed that fetch-stage immediate decode or a multi-entry BTB/BHT can crush\nFmax on Tang Nano 20K. The best predictor angle now is therefore not a more\ngeneral predictor, but the smallest predictor that attacks the hottest loop\nbranch case while keeping the PC update path almost as short as the current\npc+4/EX-redirect mux.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nAdd a one-entry loop-back branch target latch trained only by resolved taken\nconditional branches whose target is below their PC. IF consults it using a\nfull-PC tag match and a 2-bit taken counter, then chooses either pc+4 or the\nstored target without decoding imem_data and without reading a LUTRAM table.\nThe prediction metadata, including the predicted target and the stored\ninstruction word, travels with the fetched instruction so EX can verify the\nactual resolved next PC and recover on loop exits, aliases, symbolic formal\ninstruction changes, and misaligned traps. Correctly predicted hot loop-back\nbranches should avoid the current EX redirect bubble, while the hardware cost\nshould be far smaller than the failed static direct predictor or tiny BTB.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20261003-003-r5s2\nTITLE: Outstanding loop target predictor\nMOTIVATION:\nPrior predictor attempts show that CoreMark can save about four to six\npercent of cycles when hot taken control-flow bubbles are avoided, but they\nlost because target lookup and especially predicted-next-PC metadata widened\nthe timing-sensitive fetch/execute path. The closest attempt was the\nsingle-entry loop predictor; its remaining weakness was carrying predicted\ninstruction and 32-bit target fields through the pipeline just to verify a\nprediction two stages later.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nRebuild the single-entry backward-branch target predictor around one global\noutstanding-prediction record instead of per-instruction prediction payl\n…[1167 of 4239 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c8a928fa06c67d46]…\nmoving almost all verification payload and same-cycle\npredictor feedback.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nAdd a tiny exact-tag predictor for direct taken control flow, focused on\nbackward conditional branches and direct JALs. The predictor stores a full\nbranch PC tag, its deterministic target, and a small confidence bit or\ncounter; IF may use the stored target on a tag hit, but EX updates the table\nonly through registered state so training never feeds the same-cycle fetch\nlookup. Because direct branch and JAL targets are immutable for a given PC,\nthe pipeline only needs to carry a one-bit predicted_taken marker with the\ninstruction, not a 32-bit predicted target. EX suppresses the normal taken\nredirect when predicted_taken and the branch or JAL is actually taken, and\nredirects only for loop exits, unpredicted taken branches, unpredicted JALs,\nand all JALR cases. This should preserve most of the prior predictor IPC win\nwhile cutting out the wide target-compare and outstanding-record cones that\npreviously crushed Fmax.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
